# BHOOMI-NETRA Citizen — environment template
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in the values from your Supabase project settings
# (Project Settings → API). `.env` is gitignored; this template is not.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why the EXPO_PUBLIC_ prefix, and what it costs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Expo only inlines variables with this prefix into the client bundle, and it
# inlines them as plain text. The Expo docs are blunt about the consequence:
# these values "will be visible in plain-text in your compiled application".
# Anyone who downloads the APK can read both lines below.
#
# That is acceptable for exactly these two values and nothing else. A Supabase
# anon key is designed to be public; row level security is what protects the
# data, and 006_citizen_tables.sql grants the anon role SELECT on shelters, risk
# zones and routes and nothing more.
#
# Never put a service role key, a database password or an SMS gateway credential
# in this file. There is no EXPO_PUBLIC_ variable that is private, so anything
# that must stay secret belongs behind an Edge Function instead.
#
# One more Expo constraint worth knowing before you rename anything: these are
# read in src/services/supabase.ts as static `process.env.NAME` property
# accesses, because that is the only form the transform recognises. Bracket
# access and destructuring silently produce `undefined` in a release build.

EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The street map under the zone overlay
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# An XYZ raster tile template. `{z}/{x}/{y}` are required; `{s}` is optional and
# is filled from a, b, c, d. Leave the value empty to ship with no basemap at all
# — the map then draws its own graticule and says so in the credit line, which is
# a legitimate choice for a low-data build.
#
# The tile source must be a DARK style. Every mark on this map — zone labels,
# closed-shelter outlines, the guide line to your shelter — is cream on a night
# ground, and those disappear over OSM's standard light tiles. Carto's dark_all
# is the default for that reason, not for taste.
#
# Three sources worth knowing about:
#
#   Carto dark_all (the default). Free with attribution for non-commercial use.
#     https://{s}.basemaps.cartocdn.com/dark_all/{z}/{x}/{y}.png
#
#   Bhuvan (ISRO / NRSC). The right answer for an Indian government submission:
#     Indian-hosted, Survey of India lineage, no third-party dependency. Its WMTS
#     endpoint needs checking against your own account before you trust it.
#
#   OpenStreetMap standard tiles. Usable for local testing only. OSM's tile usage
#     policy does not permit app traffic at scale, and the style is light, so the
#     marks would need recolouring. If you want OSM data at scale, self-host or
#     use MapTiler.
#
# Whatever you pick, credit it. The line at the bottom of the map is rendered
# from EXPO_PUBLIC_TILE_ATTRIBUTION, so changing the source without changing the
# credit is how a licence gets breached quietly.

EXPO_PUBLIC_TILE_URL=https://{s}.basemaps.cartocdn.com/dark_all/{z}/{x}/{y}.png
EXPO_PUBLIC_TILE_ATTRIBUTION=OpenStreetMap, CARTO
